## Turn PDFs into Markdown with the document_extraction task type

1. Extract. From a PDF, get clean Markdown:

```
POST _inference/document_extraction/<eis-endpoint-id>
{
  "input": "https://example.com/elasticsearchnotes.pdf"
}

// Response: clean Markdown for the whole document 1 "
// # Elasticsearchnotes\n\n## Highlights\n...
```

2. Index and embed:

```
PUT docs
{
  "mappings": {
    "properties": {
      "content": {"type": "semantic_text" }
    }
  }
}  

POST docs/_doc
{
"title": "Elasticsearchnotes",
"Content": "# Elasticsearchnotes\n\n## Highlights\n..."
}
```

3. Retrieve:

```
GET docs/_search
{
  "Query": {
    "semantic": {
      "field": "content",
      "Query": “what’s new for OCR in 9.6?"
    }
  }
}
```

`document_extraction` is a new inference task type (in technical preview) for Elastic Inference Service (EIS) that brings the document processing capabilities of Jina Reader to Elasticsearch. When you send one request with a PDF URL, you get clean Markdown back. It also handles scans of other document types via a URL or Base64. This new task type builds upon the recently released `jina-ocr-v1` model by managing pre- and post-processing needed to feed PDFs and other document types into the model, so you make a single request and skip building a processing pipeline. You can optionally feed the output to a `semantic_text` field to handle embedding the content for search and agent workflows. To learn more, check out the detailed blog about `document_extraction` and the blog about `jina-ocr-v1`. This feature will be available as part of the 9.6 release in early December.

### Non-streaming chat completion for regulated and air-gapped deployments

Non-streaming chat completion (GA) returns a complete model response in a single synchronous reply instead of in a stream of chunks. This matters for organizations that route inference traffic through a proxy which doesn’t support HTTP streaming, such as the data loss prevention (DLP), content-inspection, and audit-logging proxies common in regulated environments. This also helps on-premises or air-gapped deployments running models locally where networking or middleware can’t support streaming connections. Elastic Agent Builder and other chat-based Kibana features now work in these deployments. This feature will be available as part of the 9.6 release in early December.

### Cut cost and latency with opt-in prompt caching

Opt-in prompt caching (in technical preview) reduces cost and latency for multi-turn agentic workflows, including Agent Builder and direct Inference API use with Anthropic models. Callers mark the portions of a request that they want cached, and session IDs improve the odds of a cache hit across turns. This works best with large, stable system prompts and tool definitions.This feature will be available as part of the 9.6 release in early December.

